Storage-node socket directory validation has a TOCTOU window and does not check setuid/setgid/sticky bits
Link: https://chatgpt.com/codex/cloud/security/findings/argmin-security-review-2026-06-23-m5
Criticality: medium (attack path: medium)
Status: fixed

# Resolution
Fixed in 203a0162.

The valid part of the finding was the special-bit gap: `validate_socket_directory` masked the parent mode to `0o777` and then checked only `mode & 0o077 == 0`, so setuid, setgid, and sticky bits were ignored. The fix now checks the full permission mode with `mode & 0o7777` and requires it to be exactly `0o700`. A regression verifies a socket directory with setgid set (`0o2700`) is rejected.

The TOCTOU and symlink portions of the original report were overstated for the intended deployment model. The storage-node process is not intended to run as root, and a non-root process cannot write into a `0700` socket directory owned by another uid. A different unprivileged local user also cannot swap entries inside that directory. Existing symlinks at the socket path were already rejected by `cleanup_stale_socket_path`, which uses `symlink_metadata` and refuses non-socket file types before stale cleanup.

An owner-uid check could still improve diagnostics and align with the control-plane socket validator, but it is not required to close the reported attack path for non-root operation. Root refusal is a broader top-level process policy decision and was not added in this fix.

